charley hardman[051127]saltypig@saltypig.com[051127]http://saltypig.com/blog/[051127]"<a href="http://www.thegunzone.com/nd.html">Just a matter of when</a>"? wrong. only if you're deficient or under fire.<BR><BR>i assume that anytime one of my guns hasn't been in my direct sight that somebody has chambered a round in it. i mean that literally. if i put the gun down and turn around to check on something, unless i'm hoping that it's loaded (e.g., at the range, with the barrel pointing downrange) i will check it again when picking it up. this has been, <i>without exception</i>, my method since i began shooting in '95. it's the method i train new shooters to adopt.<BR><BR>i have a glock 30 &mdash; the same model as in the article's ND. the glock 30 makes checking the chamber even easier. as with some other guns (my beretta 8040D is one), you can verify that the chamber's empty by aiming at something safe and bright (e.g., computer monitor, white wall, light, TV) and looking <i>through</i> the gaps in the back of the gun. if there's no round in there, you can see through the barrel. this method is superior to pulling the slide back, because pulling the slide back is the same action one uses to load a round, and it's possible to load a round when "verifying" that there isn't one there.<BR><BR>some guy pulls the trigger on a gun that hasn't been in his direct observation? idiot. moron. gun poser. these guys always want to mix themselves back into the worthy gun population after an ND, but they can't. there are few arguably excusable NDs, and that isn't one. it's not close. unless one is under fire (or strongly similar), the "matter of when" talk is BS. taking guns for granted and letting comfort degrade intelligent safety procedures (with a wide margin) is unacceptable. period.[051127]11-27-2005 15:49
