Bill St. Clair[051127]bill@billstclair.com[051127]http://billstclair.com/blog/[051127]Thanks, Charley. I agree that the article title should be a misnomer for any responsible gun owner.<BR><BR>I haven't ever had a negligent discharge either, and I look in the chamber of my under-the-bed shotgun every time I take it out of the safe, even though I'm the only one I know of with access to that safe. I also check my rifle chambers before putting them in their cases for a trip to the range, and again when I take them out at the range. And I always leave the actions open when they're laying on the bench, even if I'm the only guy at the range.<BR><BR>This guy broke two of Cooper's rules. 1. Every gun is always loaded. 2. Never cover anything with the muzzle that you don't intend to destroy. He had to break the third rule (Keep you finger off the trigger until your sights are on the target) in order to strip his gun, but had he observed the first two, there would have been no negligent discharge.<BR><BR>Still, the article is a good reminder to never get sloppy.[051127]11-27-2005 16:38
